The embedded disassembly process is integrated into the product during manufacturing and is activated at the point of disassembly using an external trigger — typically heat, electrical current, or a chemical agent. Smart materials such as shape memory alloys (SMAs) or shape memory polymers (SMPs) are used as fasteners or structural elements that change shape or lose rigidity when exposed to the trigger, causing the product to separate along pre-designed fault lines. The designer must select trigger conditions that are easily achievable in a remanufacturing facility but will not be accidentally triggered during normal product use.